      "abstract": "<jats:title>Abstract</jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>Among the fundamental requirements of VLSI-based digital signal processing systems is the improvement of filtering coefficients, which is directly connected with accuracy of frequency response, numerical stability, switching activity, and power consumption in fixed-point-based considerations. Because of this, it is of the utmost importance to design the optimization strategies of coefficients that can be jointly applied to the signal fidelity and VLSI criteria. To address such problems, the present paper proposes a COatiBAdger Hybrid Adaptive Design (COBAD) model of the FIR coefficient enhancement. In this paper, a new hybrid algorithm, the COBAD-FIR algorithm, coati optimization algorithm and honey badger algorithm to optimize the FIR filter coefficients are proposed, but with the objective of providing signal as well as VLSI efficiency. Its key contribution is a quantization- and switching-aware multi-objective fitness function that makes it possible to design FIRs with low-power and hardware-friendly designs. The introduction of quantization-sensitive and switching-activity-sensitive objectives in the optimization problem, and the ensuing coefficient generation, is one of novelty, and can now be software-implemented in hardware-efficient ways, and implemented macroscopically in VLSI technology. The performance of the suggested method is warranted by simulation. A FIR filter based on COBAD has a passband ripple of 0.028dB, stopband attenuation of 57 dB, quantization error of 0.006 and index of reduced switching activities of 0.32 that are even better than eight of the optimization methods available.</jats:p>",
